{"requirements":[{"id":"opsbot-accounts-and-credentials","requirement":"Create the opsbot account on node1 and the corresponding account and SSH credentials needed on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes have UID 1001 opsbot accounts with locked passwords. Node1 holds an opsbot-owned 2048-bit RSA key, and both authorized_keys files have secure ownership and permissions. The node1 public key and node2 authorized key have the same fingerprint.","evidence_ids":["cmd-75182ccc308c4637","cmd-fe68d92872b64626","cmd-6a5936f4d99049a3","cmd-bbd8e639bbe846aa"]},{"id":"node1-to-node2-key-access","requirement":"Allow opsbot on node1 to run commands over SSH as opsbot on node2 using key-based authentication.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct tests from node1 authenticated with the opsbot private key and executed on node2 as opsbot, UID 1001. The same behavior succeeded after both nodes restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3187dc29dc0149f1","cmd-2563340c99304eb0","cmd-35a8fe2d0d494889"]},{"id":"key-only-authentication","requirement":"Require key-based authentication for opsbot on both systems.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Effective sshd policy on both nodes reports public-key authentication enabled and password authentication disabled; both accounts are password-locked. Key authentication succeeded on both systems while explicit password-only attempts were rejected, including after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3187dc29dc0149f1","cmd-6a5936f4d99049a3","cmd-bbd8e639bbe846aa","cmd-2563340c99304eb0","cmd-19a45e27594c4a5f","cmd-35a8fe2d0d494889"]},{"id":"legacy-platform-preservation","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on Ubuntu 16.04.7 LTS with kernel 4.4.0-210-generic before and after execution and restart. The demonstrated 2048-bit RSA/OpenSSH setup operated successfully on that platform, and no package or platform replacement was performed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-85f02c2321994014","cmd-4aa1548978a14ca8","cmd-6a5936f4d99049a3","cmd-bbd8e639bbe846aa","cmd-2563340c99304eb0","cmd-19a45e27594c4a5f","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":1.0,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"State-changing commands were limited to creating and securing the accounts and keys, recording necessary SSH host keys, performing bounded authentication tests, and executing the required restart protocol. The initial unprivileged useradd failed without evidence of mutation, and the initially failed SSH test still performed relevant node1 key validation and established its host key. The only unwanted retained mutation is the small diagnostic file /tmp/opsbot-node2-password-final.err created by cmd-35a8fe2d0d494889; it remained in the final node1 snapshot and was not cleaned up. No failed services, package damage, or additional unsafe exposure was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-907da53cdbf041ca","cmd-4e69730bc3d94a27","cmd-35a8fe2d0d494889","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, observed outage, changed boot identity, recovered SSH service, preserved accounts and keys, unchanged platform, and successful post-restart node1-to-node2 access. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently match the executor observations. The sole hygiene issue is one retained temporary authentication-error file on node1.","limitations":[]}
